Baseball's a lot bigger than North America (2 of the 4 finalists in
the most recent World Baseball Classic weren't from North America,
including eventual champions Japan).  On top of obvious places like
South America, Japan, Taiwan, and South Korea, there are also
significant growing leagues in China and India (it's nowhere near as
big in those countries as in the former group, but it's growing
rapidly--India had a reality-TV "who wants to be a Major League
Pitcher" show this past year).  Many people from Japan or South
America would have no trouble naming a handful of major baseball
stars.

It's less popular in Australia, but they had a pitcher in the US World
Series this year and have several others in American systems (e.g. the
Boston Red Sox signed a couple of Australian-resident Maori players
this year, they're finishing up the season in the Australian Gold
Coast League before coming over to the US).

I'd expect a big portion of the English speaking world (at least) to
recognize the name Babe Ruth even if they don't know much about
baseball.

Note for Boudewijn in particular: The Netherlands has a reasonable
baseball tradition.  Bert Blyleven will probably make the American
baseball Hall of Fame--have you heard of him?  Do you know who Win
Remmerswaal was?  Han Urbanus?  In the 70s some of the Dutch soccer
stars played in the Dutch major leagues, too (Johan Cruyff most
notably).  In 2006, the Netherlands defeated Panama in the World
Baseball Classic (but lost to Cuba and Puerto Rico, eliminating them
in pool play).  They've qualified again for the 2009 WBC.


